# Construct a coherent sampling plan from a closed vocabulary

The input contains the original purpose, population description, sample size N, constraints and complete dimension meanings.
Dimension identifiers are the keys of sampling_dimensions.dimensions.
Categorical option identifiers are local to their dimension. Numeric ranges remain numbers, not identifiers.

Design complementary, meaningful sampling directives. For each directive, fill its sampled_values slots:
- For a categorical dimension you constrain, return a nonempty list of its allowed option identifiers as integers. Select each identifier at most once.
- For a numeric dimension, return a number or an ordered two-number range inside its supplied bounds.
- Use null for an optional dimension left unconstrained. When all dimensions are required, none can be null.

## Feasibility comes before adding coverage axes

A directive permits every selected option throughout its stated numeric ranges.
Check the youngest admissible age, not just a typical member of a broad age band.
Do not assign independently pursued hobbies, reading, cooking, gaming, smartphone habits, adult employment, or advanced education to a band that includes infants.
Leave such optional dimensions unconstrained for that band.
If the task requires a relevant distinction among older children, first separate them from infants and very young children, without losing the younger population.

For example, a band containing both infants and teenagers cannot truthfully be described as people who cook for pleasure.
A child/caregiver band with leisure interests left open is coherent; the useful cooking-interest contrast can be placed in adult bands instead.
Do not simply delete infants from a general-population plan to make a hobby axis easier.

Check all other explicit combinations too: age against age-group labels, work against labor-force status, education against age, partner status against travel party, and numeric child ages against months.
Multiple allowed values are alternatives within one dimension, but must remain compatible with the directive's other restrictions and description.
Because the caller draws each constrained dimension independently, allowing several regions
and several states does not ensure a valid state-region pair. Constrain one coherent pairing,
or leave the redundant optional dimension unconstrained. Apply the same reasoning to other
dimensions that describe the same underlying fact; the biography must not have to discard a draw.

## Coverage and quantities

Include useful demographic AND behavioral axes relevant to the purpose, using behavioral contrasts where they are appropriate.
Across the plan, actually constrain the purpose-relevant behavioral families supplied in the
dimension meanings. Merely generating a dimension or mentioning variety in prose does not
give that distinction any coverage. Spread compatible contrasts across directives rather
than leaving nearly every behavioral dimension null in favor of demographics and generic hobbies.
When the population specification explicitly lists additional dimension families, use those
families themselves: a generic hobby is not a substitute for a requested attitude, taste,
habit, personality or lifestyle contrast. Cover their contrasting supplied options where
feasible, not only the cautious or moderate option. Do not prescribe purchase decisions,
survey scores or rankings; those must emerge later from the people and the scenario.
The caller draws concrete values uniformly from your numeric ranges and uses conditional
supplied categorical weights (or equal weights) among your selected options. Design ranges
and allowed alternatives that remain coherent under those draws, not just at a typical value.

Check realized contrast coverage before returning the plan. For each behavioral
family explicitly requested by the population specification, identify the opposing
ends in the supplied option meanings, including its stated extremes rather than
substituting only moderate alternatives.
When eligibility and sample size permit, give each requested end at least one
compatible directive that selects that option alone in that dimension. Quantities
can be one; other directives may retain mixed alternatives and supplied weights.
Count only these singleton allocations as guaranteed coverage. An extreme allowed
only in a mixed list can disappear when the caller draws the actual people.
Review the completed plan against those ends and revise missing allocations before
returning it, without overriding eligibility or reported demographic proportions.
This is illustrative contrast coverage, not an estimate of behavioral prevalence.
Do not equalize demographic categories or prescribe downstream decisions or scores.

Population eligibility is mandatory in every directive, including when its corresponding
dimension is optional. State it in each subpopulation description and select explicit
eligibility values where the dimensions provide them.
A compact plan is preferable to a long inventory.
Use the original population description, not only the generated dimension summary, to identify
mandatory eligibility, explicitly requested coverage families and reported demographic proportions.
When reported marginal proportions are supplied, allocate approximately N times those proportions,
allowing for integer rounding and other constraints. A per-directive quantity cap is not a reason
to give every demographic band the same population share: split common groups across several
directives instead. Covering a rare eligible age does not require giving it a separate equal-sized
group, and illustrative coverage weights must not override reported demographic proportions.
Do not silently narrow the target population, omit supplied age groups, or discard the youngest or oldest ages merely for convenience.
For a full-population age-stratified plan, cover the supplied age range without gaps.


## Preserve weighted demographic choices when defining behavioral contrasts

When only marginal proportions are supplied, a behavioral contrast does not
establish a different demographic distribution. Within a directive, retain
the complete supplied weighted option set for each compatible nominal
demographic dimension rather than selecting one category to make the profile
distinctive. The caller can then draw from those original proportions in every
behavioral group. Restrict a demographic option set only for a stated eligibility
condition, an explicitly requested demographic stratum, or a genuine compatibility
constraint; do not invent that restriction from a personality or taste.

For example, contrasting adventurous and traditional tastes does not require
assigning different races, genders or regions to those two groups. Each group can
allow all supplied options with their original weights. This preserves expected
marginal proportions without requiring residual demographic arithmetic after each
behavioral allocation. It does not guarantee every rare demographic category
appears in a small realized sample or establish measured joint prevalence.

Where two optional dimensions encode the same demographic fact at different levels
of detail, retain one weighted description and leave the redundant dimension
unconstrained if independent draws would conflict. A full race list combined with
independently drawn race-specific cultural profiles is not coherent. Do not fix a
rare race merely to showcase a cultural profile. Cultural influences that do not
imply a particular race can still be varied. Apply the same compatibility check
to region and state, and to educational attainment and learning profiles.

Keep required age strata and supplied age proportions. Within eligible adult
bands, allow the supplied range of educational attainment unless another explicit
restriction genuinely excludes an option. Do not assign everyone in an older band
advanced education merely to produce a compact profile. An optional learning
profile can remain unconstrained rather than contradicting an allowed attainment.
Retain the requested behavioral singleton contrasts within these demographic
choices; neither a demographic label nor a survey rating is a behavioral contrast.

Compatibility still takes precedence over retaining full option sets. A job that
requires a particular qualification cannot be drawn independently of attainment
options that exclude that qualification. If the job dimension is optional, leave
it unconstrained rather than assigning everyone a degree to accommodate a possible
job. If both dimensions are required, use mutually compatible alternatives or
separate compatible directives while respecting the supplied marginal budgets.

Quantities must be positive integers summing to exactly N.
IDs must be consecutive integers starting at 1.
Respect every supplied directive-count and quantity limit. Check those numbers before returning.

Do not bundle race, gender, finances, politics and personality into unsupported stereotypes.
Supplied marginal weights can guide coverage but do not establish joint prevalence; do not claim empirical representativeness.

The caller restores complete dimension names and option strings exactly.
Interpret their full meanings rather than rewriting them.
Return only schema-conforming JSON.
Option identifiers and null are explicit intermediate representations.
Deterministic reconstruction returns the original public plan shape without repairing invalid semantic choices.
